import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,ao as n}from"./chunks/framework.CcUNt26L.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Axisymmetric Reconstruction","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"inverse/axisymmetric.md","filePath":"inverse/axisymmetric.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"inverse/axisymmetric.md"};function s(r,e,d,c,l,m){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="inverse_axisymmetric" tabindex="-1">Axisymmetric Reconstruction <a class="header-anchor" href="#inverse_axisymmetric" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Axisymmetric Reconstruction {#inverse_axisymmetric}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/api#Magnetostatics.reconstruct_axisymmetric_field"><code>reconstruct_axisymmetric_field</code></a> fits a continuous field from cylindrical probe measurements under azimuthal symmetry about the z axis. It interpolates poloidal flux and fits the toroidal field independently; it does <strong>not</strong> solve magnetohydrostatic pressure balance.</p><h2 id="Measurements-and-representation" tabindex="-1">Measurements and representation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Measurements-and-representation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Measurements and representation {#Measurements-and-representation}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><code>positions</code> is an <code>n x 2</code> matrix with rows <code>(R, Z)</code> in metres.</p></li><li><p><code>B_observed</code> is an <code>n x 3</code> matrix of <code>(B_R, B_phi, B_Z)</code> in tesla. Non-finite field components are treated as missing measurements.</p></li><li><p><code>domain = (Rmin, Rmax, Zmin, Zmax)</code> must have <code>Rmin &gt; 0</code>.</p></li><li><p><code>uncertainties</code> supplies component-wise magnetic uncertainties in tesla.</p></li></ul><p>The poloidal field derives from a radial-basis expansion of flux and is divergence-free by construction. <code>kernel = :wendland_c2</code> uses compact support; <code>:gaussian</code> uses a global basis. <code>length_scale</code>, basis centers, and regularization control the spatial model.</p><h2 id="Constraints-and-source-informed-modes" tabindex="-1">Constraints and source-informed modes <a class="header-anchor" href="#Constraints-and-source-informed-modes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Constraints and source-informed modes {#Constraints-and-source-informed-modes}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><code>boundary_positions</code> and <code>boundary_flux</code> specify exact poloidal-flux samples; <code>boundary_uncertainties</code> makes them weighted observations instead.</p></li><li><p><code>current_positions</code>, <code>current_density</code>, and <code>current_uncertainties</code> specify toroidal current density in A/m². Zero-current samples are an explicit vacuum prior, not a default physical assumption.</p></li><li><p><code>vacuum_degree</code> adds current-free polynomial modes for smooth distant-coil fields.</p></li><li><p><code>filament_positions</code> adds virtual external coaxial filaments with fitted currents. The filaments must lie outside the reconstruction domain.</p></li><li><p><code>current_element_positions</code> adds softened toroidal current elements inside the plasma region, with width controlled by <code>current_element_width</code>.</p></li></ul><p>Compactly supported flux bumps carry no net toroidal current. For a distributed plasma current, use current-carrying elements or assess whether a Gaussian basis places the return current outside the domain. Merely increasing compact-basis resolution does not supply a missing net-current mode.</p><h2 id="Evaluation-and-validation" tabindex="-1">Evaluation and validation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Evaluation-and-validation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Evaluation and validation {#Evaluation-and-validation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The returned <a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/api#Magnetostatics.AxisymmetricFieldReconstruction"><code>AxisymmetricFieldReconstruction</code></a> is called at <strong>Cartesian</strong> positions and returns Cartesian field components, despite using cylindrical measurements for fitting. <a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/api#Magnetostatics.poloidal_flux"><code>poloidal_flux</code></a> and <a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/api#Magnetostatics.current_density"><code>current_density</code></a> provide derived quantities. Evaluation outside the cylindrical domain raises <code>DomainError</code>.</p><p>Use held-out probes and compare both field and toroidal current density. Vary the basis, length scale, regularization, and source-informed modes; agreement at probes alone does not establish the inferred plasma-current distribution. Exact flux constraints use a dense null-space reduction, while uncertain boundary data permit the sparse iterative path.</p><h2 id="Example-and-API" tabindex="-1">Example and API <a class="header-anchor" href="#Example-and-API" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Example and API {#Example-and-API}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/examples/flare#flare_example">FLARE example</a> starts from known coils and then fits sparse probes, comparing vacuum, filament, and plasma-current bases. See <a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/api#Magnetostatics.reconstruct_axisymmetric_field"><code>reconstruct_axisymmetric_field</code></a> for all keywords and <a href="/Magnetostatics.jl/dev/inverse/index#inverse_methods">Choosing an inverse method</a> for alternatives when azimuthal symmetry is not appropriate.</p>',13)])])}const h=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,h as default};
